    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d212">

                <pb facs="0388" n="289" sameAs="frus:frus1936v02/pg289"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">362.1154 Spiegelberg, Betty G./13: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to the Ambassador in
                        Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dodd</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1936-09-29T15:00:00-05:00">September 29, 1936—3
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>124. With reference to your despatch 3017 and to instruction No. 581 regarding
                    the Spiegelberg case, you are requested to seek an early <pb facs="0389" n="290"
                        xml:id="pg290"/> interview with the Foreign Office, preferably with Baron
                    von Neurath<anchor n="5" xml:id="d212-f1"/> himself, for the purpose of
                    requesting him to bring about a satisfactory adjustment of this case.</p>
                <p>You should explain to him the history of the case thus far and point out that you
                    are informed that individual cases of this kind have been adjusted in the
                    past.</p>
                <p>You should also state that you do not desire to enter into a legal controversy
                    over the general principle involved, but that you do desire to request a
                    satisfactory settlement of the case itself. You should add that the fact that
                    you have been requested personally to take up the case with him indicates the
                    degree of importance which your Government attaches to a satisfactory
                    settlement.</p>
                <p>You should likewise indicate that you have been requested to submit a telegraphic
                    reply as early as practicable and that you will accordingly be grateful for an
                    early decision on the part of the German authorities.</p>
                <p>Your representations should be kept entirely oral.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d212-f1" n="5" xml:id="d212-f1-content">
                <p>German Minister for Foreign Affairs. For results of the interview, see telegram
                    No. 297, October 1, 9 a.m., from the Ambassador in Germany, <ref target="#pg301"
                        >p. 301</ref>.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
